---
/* THE PAGE THE FRONT PAGE COULD NOT BE.
 *
 * This material used to sit under the map as eight cards, each with a
 * paragraph and a list of spellings. It was cut to a single strip because
 * below a map it read as documentation of somebody else's research — half a
 * screen about families the reader has no connection to, in the place where
 * they were trying to find their own.
 *
 * The fault was the position, not the writing. A reader who has clicked
 * «My Work» is asking exactly the question the long version answers. So the
 * long version lives here, and the front page keeps the map.
 *
 * EVERY EXAMPLE ON THIS PAGE IS A REAL ARCHIVE, read from the same file the
 * strip used. Nothing here is illustrative.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import showcase from "../data/archives-showcase.json";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";

const kids = {
  id: "our-family",
  name: "Our Family",
  url: "https://daviddef.github.io/Our-Family/",
  accent: "var(--accent)",
  where: "written for children",
  what: "The landing site the eight archives meet in, written so that a "
      + "six-year-old can follow their own family back — which is the thing "
      + "people actually want, and the thing a list of parish registers does "
      + "not convey.",
};
const all = [kids, ...showcase.archives];
const names = [...new Set(showcase.archives.flatMap((a) => a.names || []))];
const eras = regions.regions.reduce((a, r) => a + r.eras.length, 0);
---
<Base
  title="My Work"
  eyebrow={`${all.length} family archives · ${names.length} surnames followed`}
  dek="Interactive family histories — the records found and cited, the claims tested, and the whole thing published as something a family can explore rather than a folder nobody opens.">

  <div class="prose" style="max-width:74ch">
    <h3>What I actually build</h3>
    <p>Not a tree. A tree is a shape, and a shape is the last thing you produce,
    not the thing you produce. What I build is an <b>archive</b>: every claim
    about a person carries the record it rests on and how sure that record makes
    it, and the whole thing is published as a site a family can walk through.</p>

    <p>The difference shows up the first time somebody disagrees with you. A tree
    says <em>Giovanni was born in 1861</em>. An archive says <em>Giovanni was born
    in 1861 — parish register of Arienzo, book 4, folio 22, which names his
    father and mother and is in his own parish's hand</em>. The first is a
    position. The second is an argument, and an argument can be checked, inherited
    and corrected by somebody who comes after you.</p>

    <h3>How it is done</h3>
    <p><b>Start from the ground, not the name.</b> Surnames move, mutate and
    repeat; ground does not. Every one of the archives below is anchored on a
    place first — Arienzo, Senj, Piedimonte Etneo, Schubin — and the people are
    hung off it. That is also why the tool on this site is a map rather than a
    search box.</p>

    <p><b>Find out whose archive it is before looking for the record.</b> This is
    the step almost everybody skips and it is the commonest reason a record that
    exists cannot be found. A 1930 baptism from a Croatian village is filed under
    <em>Italy</em>, because between the wars the village was Italian. This atlas
    carries {eras} researched eras across {regions.regions.length} regions for
    precisely that reason — <a href={u("/jurisdictions/")}>whose archive held this
    ground, and when</a>.</p>

    <p><b>Test the family stories rather than repeating them.</b> Every family has
    four or five. Some are true, some are half true, and the half-true ones are
    the most useful because the half that is wrong usually tells you where the
    record actually is. The Lerena archive exists largely to do this: four
    traditions, tested against the record one at a time.</p>

    <p><b>Write down what you did not find.</b> A search that returned nothing is
    a result, and it is the result most often thrown away — so the next person
    repeats it. Every archive here records the negative searches alongside the
    positive ones. It is the least glamorous discipline in the work and the one
    that compounds.</p>

    <p><b>Grade the claims.</b> Nothing is stated flat. A thing is proven,
    probable, a candidate, or a family story — and it says which. A candidate
    promoted quietly to a fact is how a wrong ancestor gets inherited by
    everybody who copies your tree.</p>

    <h3>Nine archives, and what each one had to solve</h3>
    <p>These follow different families on four continents, and each ran into a
    different wall. That is why there are nine of them rather than one: the
    method was worked out against problems that did not resemble each other.</p>
  </div>

  <ul class="works">
    {all.map((a) => (
      <li style={`--edge:${a.accent}`}>
        <a href={a.url} class="wn">{a.name}</a>
        <span class="ww">{a.where}</span>
        <p class="wt">{a.what}</p>
        {a.names && a.names.length > 1 && (
          <p class="ws">{a.names.join(" · ")}</p>
        )}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <div class="prose" style="max-width:74ch">
    <h3>Why this atlas exists at all</h3>
    <p>Eight of those archives kept hitting the same three walls, and all three
    were geographic: the place had changed its name, the border had moved so the
    records were in another country's archive, and "online" turned out to mean
    four or five quite different things. Solving that eight times over for eight
    families is how <a href={u("/")}>Record Atlas</a> came to exist — it is the
    part of this work that generalises, taken out and pointed at the world.</p>
    <p>The family facts stayed in the archives, where they belong. What came out
    was the geography.</p>

    <h3>If you want this done for your family</h3>
    <p>The nine sites above are what finished work looks like. <a href={u("/add/")}>
    Add a source</a> if you know a record set this map is missing — that is the
    most useful thing a stranger can do for it, and it costs nothing.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The archives carry their own colour, as they do everywhere else on this
     site. A left edge rather than a top border, because these are read as a
     list of paragraphs rather than scanned as a grid of cards. */
  .works { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 26px 0 30px;
           display: grid; gap: 14px; }
  .works li { border-left: 3px solid var(--edge, var(--rule-strong));
              background: var(--panel); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
              padding: 13px 16px 14px; }
  .wn { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 17.5px; color: var(--edge);
        text-decoration: none; }
  .wn:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .ww { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .06em;
        text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); margin-left: 10px; }
  .wt { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.6;
        color: var(--ink-2); margin: 7px 0 0; max-width: 78ch; }
  .ws { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; color: var(--ink-3);
        margin: 6px 0 0; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .ww { display: block; margin: 3px 0 0; }
  }
</style>
